    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_3686e8d215f35e1c3c6bda3a0f85d2c4c53c37e2e6c0410f95a4acd88e9ecec8",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "Changing the indicator representation moves, rather than removes, the regularity obstruction. Nonnegative convolution turns the smooth-test asymptotic into an average of translated sharp discrepancies, so one translate supplies a one-sided lower-bound witness. For two-sided control, extremal trigonometric majorants and minorants give pointwise inequalities but charge an exact constant-mode loss and expose a high-frequency zero-sum tail; band-limited smoothing elsewhere in the proof trades endpoint leakage for compact Fourier support when establishing the cancellation input used by that tail.",
